{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /**
     * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     * The guarantee. This config is the only place it holds, and
     * `pnpm typecheck` must keep invoking it.
     * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     *
     * **NO ambient types AT ALL — not `["node"]`, not `["vite/client"]`.**
     *
     * `@flux/contracts` restricts itself to the bare language so it can never
     * reference the DOM. This package has the harder constraint: it is imported
     * by `apps/web` (browser), by that app's vitest run (jsdom), and eventually
     * by a worker's suite (node). Anything ambient it relied on would work in one
     * of those and fail in the others, and the failure would land on the agent
     * that imported it rather than on the one that introduced it.
     *
     * The practical consequences, both deliberate:
     *   • no `crypto.randomUUID()` — ids are derived by a pure hash instead
     *     (determinism.ts), which is also what makes them stable
     *   • no `process.env` — a fixture that reads the environment is not a
     *     fixture
     *
     * ### Why this is a separate file rather than a line in `tsconfig.json`
     *
     * It used to be a line in `tsconfig.json`, which excluded the tests. That is
     * why it could be: a program that contains the test files **cannot** hold
     * this guarantee. `import { describe } from 'vitest'` pulls `@vitest/*`
     * declarations that reference `@types/node`, and an ambient type reached by
     * any file in a program is available to every file in it — so the tests
     * supply `process` to the whole package whether or not `types` mentions it.
     * Measured both ways: `types: []` over source and tests exits 0, and
     * excluding the tests from the same config brings TS2591 back.
     *
     * The tests had to be in the config a language server discovers, or they get
     * checked in an inferred project with no `strict` and nobody can see it. So
     * they went there and this file was split off to keep the guarantee.
     *
     * **`exclude` is load-bearing.** If this config ever stops excluding
     * `src/**\/*.test.ts`, it gains node types by proxy and becomes a no-op that
     * passes — the exact "green tick either way" shape CLAUDE.md warns about.
     * Likewise, dropping `tsc -p tsconfig.src.json` from the `typecheck` script
     * loses the guarantee outright with nothing failing.
     */
    "noEmit": true,
    /** composite + noEmit are incompatible; this config only ever checks. */
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "types": []
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["src/**/*.test.ts", "dist", "node_modules"]
}
